      "writtenOn": "2022-08-04T01:08:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we do any color space transformations right now in graphite. In ganesh, the color space transformations could be handled either in CPU (if we knew it was a constant color, or a the color stops for gradients), or we\u0027d add extra shader code to transform the output from some source (e.g. a texture or the vertices) to the render target\u0027s color space.\n\nBy default, the effect system assumes every step returns a color matching the render target (or dst) color space, and generally operates in the dst color space. For the finite effects (color/gradient) we chose to compute that on the CPU so there\u0027d be no additional shader generated. But when the source color comes from something with lots of colors, we have to update the generated shader to account for it.\n\nI imagine that graphite will operate in a very similar way, but that does mean we will need to provide sufficient information to the RenderStep to handle primitive color space management, and right now that\u0027s completely dropped on the floor. A todo is totally reasonable in order to move the rest of it forward.",
      "parentUuid": "aa47bcdf_b8987228",
      "revId": "145345982e7507f9e8b9386b25bd46b36b0c705d",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    }
